 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "**Mechanism:** a single `ContextVar` plus werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects that resolve lazily on each access.\n\n- `src/tessel/globals.py:40` defines `_app_var: ContextVar[AppContext]`.\n- `src/tessel/globals.py:44-62` creates the proxies, all bound to that same `_app_var`:\n  - `active_app` = `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`, the `.app` attribute of the current context.\n  - `g` = `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`, the `.g` attribute.\n  - `request` = `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`, the `.request` attribute.\n  - `session` is the same pattern, and `app_ctx` proxies the context itself.\n- `src/tessel/ctx.py:260` defines the `AppContext` class that holds `app`, `g`, `request` and `session`.\n  - `ctx.py:433` calls `_app_var.set(self)` when the context is pushed.\n  - `ctx.py:498` calls `_app_var.reset(self._cv_token)` when it is popped.\n\nEach proxy access reads the context var for the current thread or coroutine and returns that attribute of the current `AppContext`. If nothing is bound, it raises the `_no_app_msg` / `_no_req_msg` \"Working outside of ... context\" errors. For `request` and `session`, which are only set when a request is active, the error is `_no_req_msg`.",
